### Step 1: Identify the given reaction and calculate \(\Delta n_{g}\)

The given reaction is:
SO_{2}(g) + \frac{1}{2}O_{2}(g) \rightleftharpoons SO_{3}(g)
The change in the number of moles of gaseous species (\(\Delta n_{g}\)) is calculated as:
\Delta n_{g} = (\text{moles of gaseous products}) - (\text{moles of gaseous reactants})
\Delta n_{g} = 1 - (1 + \frac{1}{2}) = 1 - \frac{3}{2} = -\frac{1}{2}

### Step 2: List the given values and convert temperature to Kelvin

Given:
K_{p} = 2 \times 10^{12}
P = 1 \text{ atm}
T = 27^{\circ}C = 27 + 273 = 300 \text{ K}
R = 0.082 \text{ L atm K}^{-1} \text{ mol}^{-1}

### Step 3: Use the relation between \(K_{p}\) and \(K_{c}\) to find \(K_{c}\)

The relationship between \(K_{p}\) and \(K_{c}\) is:
K_{p} = K_{c}(RT)^{\Delta n_{g}}
Rearranging to solve for \(K_{c}\):
K_{c} = \frac{K_{p}}{(RT)^{\Delta n_{g}}} = K_{p}(RT)^{-\Delta n_{g}}
Substitute the values:
K_{c} = (2 \times 10^{12}) \times (0.082 \times 300)^{-(-\frac{1}{2})}
K_{c} = (2 \times 10^{12}) \times (24.6)^{\frac{1}{2}}
K_{c} = (2 \times 10^{12}) \times \sqrt{24.6}
Calculate the square root of 24.6:
\sqrt{24.6} \approx 4.9598
K_{c} = (2 \times 10^{12}) \times 4.9598
K_{c} = 9.9196 \times 10^{12}

### Step 4: Express \(K_{c}\) in the required format and round to the nearest integer

We need to express \(K_{c}\) in the format \(\times 10^{13}\):
K_{c} = 0.99196 \times 10^{13}
Rounding \(0.99196\) to the nearest integer gives \(1\).
K_{c} = 1 \times 10^{13}

The osmotic pressure equation is given by:
\pi = \text{CRT}
Where \(\pi\) is osmotic pressure, \(C\) is molar concentration, \(R\) is the gas constant, and \(T\) is temperature.
We are given:
• Temperature \(T = 27^{\circ}\text{C} = 27 + 273 = 300\text{ K}\)
• Mass of solute \(w = 2.5\text{ g}\)
• Volume of solution \(V = 250.0\text{ mL} = 250/1000\text{ L}\)
• Osmotic pressure \(\pi = 400\text{ Pa}\)
• Gas constant \(R = 0.083\text{ L bar K}^{-1}\text{ mol}^{-1}\)
First, convert osmotic pressure from Pa to bar. \(1\text{ bar} = 10^5\text{ Pa}\).
\pi = \frac{400\text{ Pa}}{10^5\text{ Pa/bar}} = 4 \times 10^{-3}\text{ bar}
The molar concentration \(C\) is given by \(C = \frac{w}{M_0 V}\), where \(M_0\) is the molar mass of the solute.
Substitute the values into the osmotic pressure equation:
\frac{400\text{Pa}}{10^5} = \frac{2.5\text{g}}{M_{0}} \times \frac{1}{250/1000\text{ L}} \times 0.083\frac{\text{L-bar}}{\text{Kmol}} \times 300\text{K}
Rearrange to solve for \(M_0\):
M_{0} = \frac{2.5\text{g} \times 0.083\frac{\text{L-bar}}{\text{Kmol}} \times 300\text{K}}{\frac{400\text{Pa}}{10^5\text{ Pa/bar}} \times \frac{250}{1000}\text{ L}}
M_{0} = \frac{2.5 \times 0.083 \times 300}{4 \times 10^{-3} \times 0.25}
M_{0} = \frac{62.25}{0.001}
M_{0} = 62250
The molar mass of the solute is \(62250\text{ gmol}^{-1}\).

