Let E be the event that two balls drawn are black. Let \(A_i\) be the event that the bag contains i black balls. Since two black balls are drawn, the bag must contain at least 2 black balls (i.e., \(i \in \{2, 3, 4, 5, 6\}\)). We want to find the probability that the bag contains at least 5 black balls, given that two black balls were drawn. This is \(P(A_5 \cup A_6 | E)\).
Using the concept of conditional probability (Bayes' Theorem), assuming each initial composition is equally likely:
\text{Probability} = \frac{P(E|A_5) + P(E|A_6)}{P(E|A_2) + P(E|A_3) + P(E|A_4) + P(E|A_5) + P(E|A_6)}
This simplifies to the ratio of the number of ways to choose 2 black balls for the favorable cases to the total number of ways for all possible cases.
\text{Probability} = \frac{^{5}C_{2} + ^{6}C_{2}}{^{2}C_{2} + ^{3}C_{2} + ^{4}C_{2} + ^{5}C_{2} + ^{6}C_{2}}
Calculating the values of the combinations:
= \frac{10+15}{1+3+6+10+15}
= \frac{25}{35}
= \frac{5}{7}

The general term \(T_{r+1}\) in the expansion of \(\left(x^{\frac{2}{3}} + \frac{2}{x^3}\right)^{30}\) is given by:
T_{r+1} = {^{30}C_r} \left(x^{\frac{2}{3}}\right)^{30-r} \left(\frac{2}{x^3}\right)^r
= {^{30}C_r} x^{\frac{2(30-r)}{3}} 2^r x^{-3r}
= {^{30}C_r} 2^r x^{\frac{60-2r}{3} - 3r}
= {^{30}C_r} 2^r x^{\frac{60-2r-9r}{3}}
= {^{30}C_r} 2^r x^{\frac{60-11r}{3}}
We are given that the term is of the form \(\beta x^{-\alpha}\), where \(\alpha > 0\) is the smallest number. This means the exponent of \(x\) must be negative.
\frac{60-11r}{3} < 0
60-11r < 0
60 < 11r
r > \frac{60}{11}
Since \(r\) must be an integer, the smallest integer value for \(r\) satisfying this condition is \(r = 6\) (as \(60/11 \approx 5.45\)).
For \(r=6\), the term is \(T_{6+1} = T_7\).
T_7 = {^{30}C_6} 2^6 x^{\frac{60-11(6)}{3}}
T_7 = {^{30}C_6} 2^6 x^{\frac{60-66}{3}}
T_7 = {^{30}C_6} 2^6 x^{\frac{-6}{3}}
T_7 = {^{30}C_6} 2^6 x^{-2}
Comparing this with \(\beta x^{-\alpha}\), we have:
\beta = {^{30}C_6} \times 2^6
Since \(^{30}C_6\) is an integer and \(2^6\) is an integer, \(\beta\) is an integer, so \(\beta \in \mathbb{N}\).
And from the exponent of \(x\), we have:
-\alpha = -2
\alpha = 2
This value of \(\alpha = 2\) is positive, satisfying \(\alpha > 0\).

